```sas
ods pdf file="journal.pdf" startpage=no;
proc print data=sasuser.data;
  title "Journal Format";
  footnote "Page &bPageDate of &pchapters";
  format pageno compress;
  linesize 80;
  pagesize 11in 8.5in;
run;
ods pdf close;
```
Let me explain what each part does:

* `ods pdf file="journal.pdf" startpage=no;`: This statement tells SAS to output the report to a PDF file named "journal.pdf" and to start the PDF output immediately (without creating a new page).
* `proc print data=sasuser.data;`: This statement executes a PROC PRINT procedure, which prints the contents of the SAS data set "sasuser.data".
* `title "Journal Format";`: This statement sets the title of the report to "Journal Format".
* `footnote "Page &bPageDate of &pchapters";`: This statement sets the footnote of the report to "Page &bPageDate of &pchapters", where `&bPageDate` is the current date and `&pchapters` is the current page number.
* `format pageno compress;`: This statement formats the page number as a compressed integer (e.g., "1" instead of "Page 1 of 1").
* `linesize 80;`: This statement sets the line size to 80 characters per line.
* `pagesize 11in 8.5in;`: This statement sets the page size to 11 inches wide and 8.5 inches tall.
* `run;`: This statement executes the PROC PRINT procedure.
* `ods pdf close;`: This statement closes the PDF output.

When you run this code, SAS will output a PDF file named "journal.pdf" in the journal format, with the specified title, footnote, and page size.

